<!--
    A store to a global before a load through a pointer that may point at the
    global is kept when the global is stored again after the load (P3
    Heritage::guard, option indexaliasguard global; GitHub issue #825).

    Heritage gives a pointer LOAD no read of any global, so in

        int d_loop(int *p, int n) { int s = 0;
          for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { gi = gi + 1; s += *p; gi = gi - 1; } return s; }

    nothing reads the first store's COPY and ActionDeadCode deletes it: kuna
    printed the loop with no store to gi at all, so with p == &gi the printed
    loop sums 4 three times where the binary sums 5.  At `global` heritage puts
    an addrforce COPY read of the global in front of each such LOAD, so the
    store that reaches the load is kept where the binary makes it.  d_branch
    stores before a branch whose arm loads and stores again, d_pre before a
    loop of loads, and d_cond (clang) before a branch that loads.  A kept store
    stays where the binary makes it, so a load whose value is live across a
    store to a global keeps its own variable: d_load, the issue's
    `gi = a; x = *p; gi = b; return x;`, printed `gi = a2; return *a1;`.  d_phi,
    whose second store is computed from a value stored by the first, and d_mv,
    whose load is stored to gi after a store to gj, must keep the output they
    already had at `global`.

    Each pass runs `load` (the defect) and then `global` (the default).  The
    wrapper only supplies the harness architecture (`load file` needs one).
    The pass loads globalloadguard_gcc_O2_x86_64 and
    globalloadguard_clang_O2_x86_64 (built from globalloadguard_x86_64.c
    beside them).
-->
